-- ecosystems
Shoreline plants stabilising soil
Fish nurseries in sheltered shallows', 'Lake Kivu, western Rwanda', '{"src":"/images/photos/lake-kivu.jpg","alt":"Forested shoreline and calm water of Lake Kivu, Rwanda","credit":"Muhirehussein12, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"}'::jsonb, 1, true),
  ('0ed5e504-cb66-4814-ac54-e38c17cd8a8c', 'montane-rainforest', 'Montane rainforest', 'montane_forest', 'About 1,600 to 2,950 m', 'Cool, wet mountain forest of the Albertine Rift, rich in species found nowhere else.', '<p>Rwanda''s mountain forests grow along the high ridge that divides the Congo and Nile river basins, and on the slopes of the Virunga volcanoes. They are part of the Albertine Rift, a region recognised for its exceptional number of endemic species.</p><p>These forests capture rain and mist and release water slowly, feeding streams that communities, farms and wetlands depend on far downstream.</p>', 'Capturing rainfall and feeding rivers
Seed dispersal by birds and primates
Storing carbon in trees and soils', 'Nyungwe, Gishwati-Mukura and Volcanoes National Parks', '{"src":"/images/photos/nyungwe-forest.jpg","alt":"Montane rainforest trees in Nyungwe Forest National Park, Rwanda","credit":"Brian Harries, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons"}'::jsonb, 2, true),
  ('00e5de44-f3d9-4594-ab52-e78c58ab768c', 'hills-and-farmland', 'Hills and farmland', 'hills', 'About 1,500 to 2,000 m', 'The cultivated hills where most people live, and where everyday biodiversity is closest to hand.', '<p>Much of Rwanda is a landscape of terraced hills, fields, homesteads, school grounds and scattered trees. It is easy to overlook, but it is full of life: pollinating insects, garden birds, wild plants along paths and field edges.</p><p>For most young people this is where an interest in nature begins. A school garden, a hedgerow or a single fig tree can be a place to learn how to observe carefully.</p>', 'Pollination of crops and wild plants
Trees and hedges reducing soil erosion
Soil organisms recycling nutrients', 'Rwanda''s central plateau', '{"src":"/images/photos/rwanda-hills-sunrise.jpg","alt":"Sunrise over layered forested hills in Nyungwe, Rwanda","credit":"Jonkmania, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"}'::jsonb, 3, true),
  ('56b998ad-6b6a-409d-a465-aff53fde56f5', 'wetlands-and-marshes', 'Wetlands and marshes', 'wetland', 'About 1,300 to 2,100 m', 'Valley marshes, papyrus swamps and highland peatlands that store and clean water.', '<p>Wetlands fill many of Rwanda''s valley bottoms, from papyrus swamps along lowland rivers to the highland Rugezi marsh, which is recognised as a wetland of international importance under the Ramsar Convention.</p><p>Wetlands slow floodwater, filter sediment, hold carbon in waterlogged soils and give refuge to cranes, kingfishers, frogs and many other species. They are also among the habitats under greatest pressure.</p>', 'Storing water and reducing floods
Filtering sediment and nutrients
Locking carbon away in peat', 'Rugezi marsh, Akanyaru and Nyabarongo wetlands', '{"src":"/images/photos/rugezi-wetland.jpg","alt":"Channels of water winding through the green Rugezi wetland beneath rolling hills","credit":"Bisoke, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"}'::jsonb, 4, true),
  ('ba18a894-a7ca-4ac4-a0f6-6cfc0c6f13fc', 'savanna-and-eastern-lakes', 'Savanna and eastern lakes', 'savanna', 'About 1,250 to 1,800 m', 'Open grassland, woodland and a chain of lakes and swamps in Rwanda''s warmer east.', '<p>Eastern Rwanda is lower, warmer and drier. Grassland and acacia woodland run down to lakes and papyrus swamps along the Akagera River.</p><p>Large grazing and browsing animals shape this landscape. By feeding, trampling and moving seeds and nutrients, they keep a shifting mosaic of grass, shrubs and trees that many other species rely on.</p>', 'Grazing and browsing shaping vegetation
Large animals moving seeds and nutrients
Seasonal cycles of rain, growth and fire', 'Akagera National Park', '{"src":"/images/photos/akagera-waterbirds.jpg","alt":"Cormorants perched on small islands in calm water fringed by papyrus in Akagera National Park","credit":"John Cooke, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons"}'::jsonb, 5, true)
